    11	
    12	## What is registered
    13	
    14	- **Parameters** (A9.2; frozen profile §1): δ = 72 h, ε = 24 h, k = 6, N = 3, S = 24 h.
    15	- **Attempts** (A2.3, `:259-268`): at most N. Each has its own declared issue time. A retry that changes any
    16	  committed byte needs a fresh stamp (A9.5, `phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:295`), and its signatures are frozen
    17	  before it is stamped (A9.5, `:290-296`).
    18	- **Eligibility** (A2.1 `:91-94`; A2.2 `:220-224`; A3.7.3 `:659-673`): the attempt's own authenticated evidence for
    19	  `SHA256(A_sig)`, with the lowest qualifying height buried k = 6 deep on the pinned, linked segment (profile §7.1),
    20	  satisfies all three conjuncts: `declared − ε ≤ T`, `T ≤ declared + δ`, and `confirmed_at ≤ declared + δ`. This
    21	  is narrower than the whole `VALID_STRICT` verdict, and the remaining pre-ship checks stay separate. Rows 12.2 and
    22	  12.3 of the verifier are two of the conjuncts, not the predicate.
    23	- **Latch and expiry** (A2.1 `:150-162`): eligibility observed at or before `B = declared + δ + S` is timely. The
    24	  boundary is inclusive and "latch-winning", and a latched attempt cannot expire. Expiry applies only strictly
    25	  after B, and only to attempts with no timely-latched eligibility. Atomicity of latch against expiry is an
    26	  obligation on the implementation (A2.3). If the predicate fails at issuance time, the attempt is discarded and
    27	  re-issued, subject to N (A2.1 `:161-162`). A lag beyond S *may* be refused (A2.1 `:135-138`); refusing it is not
    28	  required.
    29	- **Refusal** (A2.3 `:265-268, :279-281`): exhausting N ends issuance in an explicit refusal, durably recorded, and
    30	  the transition that expires the final attempt records the refusal in the same step. In V1 the refusal is local and
    31	  returned by the API (A9.7 row 12; `docs/V1-CLARIFICATIONS.md` CL-03). A3's portable record, commitment and
    32	  publication are not V1.
    33	- **Budget** (A2.3 `:271-274, :288-289`): N × (δ + S), twelve days, is a *nominal* budget, "not a proven wall-clock
    34	  bound". Termination is an obligation on the implementation, not a proven liveness property.
    35	- **Window** (A9 `:422`): an attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped.
    36	- **No SLA here** (A2.1 `:139-142`): a hard latency bound may enter only as a deployment-profile item with monitoring
    37	  and an explicit violation outcome. It never kills an eligible attempt.
    38	
    39	## What the issuer does (revision 3; steps 3-6 and 10 revision 4 after `CODEX-CONFIRM-2.md`; steps 5, 8 and 10 revision 5, step 5 revisions 5.1 and 5.2, after the TLA+ model)
    40	
    41	The second revision let any worker latch or expire, and relied on compare-and-set plus re-observation. Codex found
    42	interleavings in which a stale or partial fetch expires an attempt that another, stalled worker had already observed
    43	on time (ILC-01). Compare-and-set makes the writes exclusive. It does not make the earlier observation win. Revision 3
    44	removes the concurrency instead of arbitrating it.
    45	
    46	1. **One writer.** Every lifecycle transition (start an attempt, latch, discard, expire, refuse, complete) is made
    47	   only by the poller, which runs with reserved concurrency 1 (`service/issuer/infra/main.tf:354`). The API is
    48	   read-only for the lifecycle: `api.status` no longer calls `settle` (`api.py:194`). Every transition is a write
    49	   guarded on the expected state *and* attempt id, so a write meant for an ended attempt fails (ILC-05).
    50	2. **Attempt isolation.** As before: each attempt's declared time, frames, signature jobs, proofs and evidence live
    51	   under its id. A late response names its attempt and can never fill another.
    52	3. **Latch (revision 4).** On each poll, for each open attempt, the poller fetches and upgrades the attempt's
    53	   evidence and evaluates the predicate. If it holds, the poller writes the latch with `observed_at` and the
    54	   qualifying presentation itself, not only its digests. A latch may be written at any time while the attempt is
    55	   open, however late the observation. Timeliness is a chain fact (the registered model's `burialAtWall`,
    56	   `formal/tla/P5cP5P6_BridgeSlack_Latch.tla:49, :68-71`), not the moment of the issuer's write.
    57	4. **Expiry only on a conclusive negative (revision 4, after `CODEX-CONFIRM-2.md` ILC2-01 to ILC2-03).** Absence of
    58	   evidence is never evidence of absence. An unlatched attempt expires only when, after B, the poller's own evidence
    59	   for it is **complete** and fails the predicate. Complete means three things:
    60	   - every calendar the attempt was stamped to has returned a Bitcoin attestation;
    61	   - for every attestation, the full pinned, linked `h…h+5` segment has been obtained (profile §7.1,
    62	     `270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:879-891`);
    63	   - so the lowest qualifying height, its T and its confirmation are fixed and fail a conjunct.
    64	
    65	   Stale, pending, partial or unavailable evidence never expires an attempt. No lease timing is relied on: whatever
    66	   a killed invocation saw, a later invocation will see at least the same complete evidence. Calendars do not
    67	   retract, the chain is public, and shallower reorganisations are absorbed by k-deep burial.
    68	5. **Evidence that stays unobtainable (revision 5, after the TLA+ model, `formal/tla/issuer-lifecycle/RESULTS.md`).**
    69	   An attempt whose evidence cannot be completed stays open. "Cannot be completed" includes sources that are up but
    70	   answer with stale or partial data, not only outages (R5-F4). Revision 5.1 applies the R5 model findings:
    71	   - **Fence first (R5-F3).** Before an invocation fetches for an attempt, it makes a guarded write that bumps the
    72	     attempt's version, and continues only after that write commits. A write left in flight by a killed earlier
    73	     invocation then fails its guard, so a leftover ending write can no longer beat a live latch.
    74	   - **Reading taken before the fetch (R5-F1).** `unavailable_since` takes the wall reading made *before* the fetch
    75	     starts, and only fetches started after B count.
    76	   - **Reset only by a committed success (R5-F2).** A poll whose fetch succeeds (complete, or showing the predicate
    77	     holding) clears `unavailable_since` and `failed_polls` in its committed write. A success that never commits
    78	     resets nothing. That is the attainable form of the rule, as the model checks it.
    79	   - **Sustained failure, counted by interval (revision 5.2, after R5.1 finding 1).** The period after
    80	     `unavailable_since` is divided into poll intervals. A failed poll records the index of the interval its fetch
    81	     started in, in `failed_intervals`, a set, so each interval counts at most once however many polls land in it.
    82	     An *unlatched* attempt may end with `EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE` only when both hold:
    83	     - `now − unavailable_since` ≥ the operating period;
    84	     - `failed_intervals` contains every interval of that period, less a stated allowance.
    85	
    86	     A burst of polls in one interval cannot stand in for an interval with no poll. A committed success clears the
    87	     set.
    88	   - **Every lifecycle write is version-guarded (R5.1 finding 3).** That covers the fence, the latch, the
    89	     `failed_intervals` update, the ending write, and the end-and-allocate write. A retried or leftover update
    90	     therefore cannot count a failure twice or act on a version it did not read.
    91	   - The ending decision re-fetches immediately before its guarded write.
    92	
    93	   The basis is A2.1's permission, "an attempt whose lag exceeds S may be refused" (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:135-138`),
    94	   together with A2.3's termination obligation (`:288-289`). A latched attempt is never ended for unavailability. An
    95	   attempt that is not the last ends this way, and the next one starts (step 8). This is the usual end for an
    96	   attempt that never confirms, because only complete evidence can expire it (model finding 5).
    97	
    98	   **The residual, as the model found it (finding 2).** A refused attempt might have been on time on three paths:
    99	   - (a) An invocation observed it on time, then was killed before writing, and the evidence then stayed
   100	     unobtainable for the whole period.
   101	   - (b) Nobody observed it before the evidence became unobtainable for the whole period.
   102	   - (c) A killed invocation's leftover ending write commits before a live invocation's latch.
   103	
   104	   Revision 5.2 narrows (a) and (b) to *sustained* failure: a failed poll in every interval of the period. It closes
   105	   (c) with the fence write; the R5.1 model confirms (c) unreachable, and reachable again without the fence. It does not remove (a) or (b). Whether A2.1's permission covers (b) depends on what A2 means by B. Its prose
   106	   reads B as the service's observation time (`:115-124`); its mechanized model uses the time the burial happened
   107	   (`formal/tla/P5cP5P6_BridgeSlack_Latch.tla:49, :68-71`). Paths (a) and (c) are not covered under either reading.
   108	   **Accepting this residual is an author decision**, because it means V1 does not fully meet "every
   109	   timely-eligible attempt ships" (A2.1 `:143-145`). It goes to the author with this narrowed statement.
   110	6. **Permanent failure before B.** The same rule as step 4: discard before B only on complete evidence that fails
   111	   a conjunct.
   112	7. **After the latch.** The preserved qualifying presentation is used for assembly; it is never swapped for a
   113	   different, worse one. A transient failure (store, explorer, calendar, DNS snapshot) is retried. The attempt is
   114	   discarded only for a failure the frozen attempt cannot cure even with recovered evidence: T of the preserved
   115	   presentation > not-after (A9 `:422`), or a verifier reason that depends only on the attempt's committed bytes.
   116	   Each discard records its reason (ILC-04).
   117	8. **Next attempt and refusal.** Ending attempt i and allocating attempt i+1 happen in one guarded write. So a crash
   118	   cannot leave the request between attempts, and a retry cannot run twice. Each ended attempt records
   119	   its own reason: `EXPIRED` (complete negative evidence after B), `DISCARDED` (complete negative evidence before B,
