    11	(*                                                                          *)
    12	(* R51-1 FENCE (R5-F3). After Start reads (state, attempt id, version,     *)
    13	(*   unavailable_since, failed_polls), the invocation sends a fence write *)
    14	(*   guarded on (state, attempt id, version read) that bumps the version, *)
    15	(*   and continues ONLY after it commits; then its own version is the     *)
    16	(*   bumped one. If the fence fails its guard (or is dropped) the         *)
    17	(*   invocation ends. Every later write of the invocation is guarded on    *)
    18	(*   (state, attempt id, its fenced version) and bumps the version.        *)
    19	(*   (The design names the version guard for the fence and the ending     *)
    20	(*   write; this model applies it to every write — see R51 findings.)     *)
    21	(* R51-2 READING BEFORE THE FETCH (R5-F1). The fetch's wall reading `fat` *)
    22	(*   is taken as the fetch starts; only a fetch with fat > B counts as a  *)
    23	(*   failed poll; unavailable_since := fat of the first counted failure.  *)
    24	(* R51-3 RESET ONLY BY A COMMITTED SUCCESS (R5-F2). A successful fetch    *)
    25	(*   (complete, or showing the predicate) sends a latch or an end, whose  *)
    26	(*   commit clears unavailable_since and failed_polls (and leaves the     *)
    27	(*   attempt not open). A success that never commits resets nothing.     *)
    28	(* R51-4 SUSTAINED FAILURE. Each counted failed poll sends a `fail` write *)
    29	(*   (sets unavailable_since if unset, increments failed_polls, saturating*)
    30	(*   at Expected + 1). EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE needs, on the values read:    *)
    31	(*   now - unavailable_since >= OpPeriod AND failed_polls >= Expected -   *)
    32	(*   Allowance (Expected = OpPeriod \div PollInterval), then the deciding *)
    33	(*   poll's own fetch fails and its immediate re-fetch fails.             *)
    34	(* R51-5 POLL RATE. Constant MaxPollsPerTick: at most that many poller   *)
    35	(*   starts per time unit. 1 = the EventBridge schedule taken as a hard   *)
    36	(*   rate; 2 = a burst (EventBridge redelivery, Lambda async retry, a     *)
    37	(*   loop). It must be finite: every poll's fence bumps the version, so   *)
    38	(*   unbounded polls per tick make the state space infinite. The design's *)
    39	(*   "failed_polls >= the number of scheduled polls" presumes 1.          *)
